Politicians in Wales Afraid To "Like Fools"
Disreputes among certain members of the Welsh National Assembly over a proposal that their title should be changed to MWPs (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
It has arisen because of plans to change its name to Welsh Parliament (the Welsh parliament).
MAMs from across the political spectrum are concerned that the name change could give rise to ridicule.
One member from the Labour Party said his group was concerned because "it rhymes on Twp and Pwp."
To Readers Outside of Wales: In Welsh, twp means smirking, and pwp means shit.
A member from the party Plaid Cymru said that the group as a whole "was not satisfied" and had proposed alternatives.
A Welsh Conservative said his group was "opened" to the name change, but noted that there is only a short verbal leap from MWP to Muppet (fool).
In this context the Welsh letter w is pronounced in the same way that the letter u is pronounced in Yorkshire-English.
The Assembly Commission, which currently puts together a bill imposing the name changes, said: "The final decision on denominations that members of the parliament will get is, of course, up to the members themselves."
The Government of the Wales Act 2017 gave the Welsh National Congregation to change its name.
In June, the commission published the results of a public consultation of the proposals in which there was broad support to call the Assembly a Welsh parliament (Welsh Parliament).
With respect to the title of the AMs, the commission preferred Welsh Parliament Members (Welsh MEPs) or WMPs, but the MWP opportunity got the most backing in a public consultation.
AMs apparently propose alternative options, but the struggle to achieve agreement could prove difficult for the chairman of the assembly, Elin Jones, which is expected to put together a bill on the changes within a few weeks.
