Makedoners hold voting on changing the country's name
The Macedonian's people voted on Sunday about whether their country's name should be changed to "Republic of North Macedonia," a measure that would solve a decades-old stride with Greece, which has blocked Macedonia's attempts to achieve membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece has a provincation called Macedonia and maintains that the name of their neighboring country to the north constitutes a claim on Greek territory, and has therefore used their right of veto when Macedonia has sought entry into NATO and the European Union.
The two governments reached an agreement in June based on the proposed new name but nationalist adversaries believe the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia's primarily Slavic population.
President Gjorge Ivanov has said he will not take part in the vote, and a boycott campaign has raised doubts as to whether the electorate will make up the 50 per cent required for the vote to be valid.
The question on the ballot was: "Are you in favour of NATO and EU membership with the acceptance of the agreement with Greece?"
Entrepreneurises of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, believe it is a price worth paying to create access to associations such as the EU and NATO for Macedonia, one of the countries that aroused as a result of the collapse of Yugoslavia.
"I came today to vote about the country's future, for young people in Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the EU's umbrella, because it means safer lives for all of us," Olivera Georgijevska, 79, said in Skopje.
The vote is not legally binding, but arguably members of the parliament have said that they will comply with the result of the vote and make it in effect.
The naming change would require a two-slaughter majority in parliament.
The State Electoral Commission said no irregularities had been reported.
But the turnout was at just 16 per cent compared to 34 per cent in the last general election in 2016, when 66 per cent of registered voters voted.
"I voted today because of my children. We belong in Europe", said Gjose Tanevski, 62, a consignee in the capital Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko took part in the vote in Macedonia for a change to the country's name, which would open up membership of NATO and the European Union, in Strumica in Macedonia on September 30, 2018.
In front of parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, prepared a small stage and pulled up chairs in front of the tents that were lined up by those who will boycott the vote.
"We are for NATO and the European Union, but we are going to achieve membership with raised forehead, not by the back door," Kavadarkov said.
"We are a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don't want us as Macedonia, we can turn to others like China and Russia and become part of European-Asian integration."
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO membership would provide a much-needed-backed investment in Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate of over 20 per cent.
"I think a vast majority will vote in favour because over 80 per cent of our citizens are advocating for the European Union and NATO," Zaev said after casting his vote.
He said an "yes" result would be an "enassurance of our future."
An opinion poll that was released last Monday by Macedonia's Institute for Political Research indicated that between 30 and 43 per cent of voters would take part in the vote - fewer than the required turnout.
Another poll conducted by the Macedonian television channel Telma TV indicated that 57 per cent of those surveyed planned to vote on Sunday.
Out of these, 70 per cent said they'd vote yes.
In order for the vote to be valid, the turnout must be at least 50 per cent plus one vote.
If the vote fails, it will be the first major political loss for the provincial government since it occurred in May last year.
