In relation to the importance of a responsible economic policy, in relation to national security, in Europe, ironically similar issues that Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders in.
The convening of the Nottingham East Labour meeting on Friday stated that "we want the meetings to be inclusive and prolific".
For the majority of my eight-year-old as a local member of parliament, the meetings on Friday night have just been like this.
Today, unfortunately, the tone of many meetings isn't like that, and the promise of "feciferous, gentler" politics lies way back in the past if it ever got started.
It's becoming increasingly apparent that dissenting views aren't tolerated in the Labour and that all opinions are weighed according to whether the party's leadership can accept them.
It started a short time after Jeremy became a leader when colleagues who I previously thought I shared a political standpoint with began to expect that I was going to make a U-rejugment and take positions that I would otherwise never have agreed with – whether it was about national security or the EU's internal market.
When I say my opinion in the public – and it doesn’t really matter what I’m saying – a long series of crurge messages on the social media that encourage the opt-out and opposition to middle-appearing politics and tells me I shouldn’t be a member of Labour.
And that's not just my experience.
In fact, I'm luckier than some of my colleagues, because the comments directed at me are usually political.
"I have deep respect for the professionalism and the fight of the colleagues who are lavished every day with sexism or racism, but still continue.
One of the most disappropriate aspects of this political era is how normal abuse has become.
Jeremy Corbyn said last-a-week that Labour should create a tolerant culture.
But, in reality, it's no longer an inclusive organisation and with every motion of censure or change selection rules the party becomes narrower.
I've got lots of advice over the past two years that if I put a lid on myself and am not so loud, then I'll "enough get by".
But that's not why I went into politics.
Since I became a member of the Labour 32-Years Ago when I went to school, I was provoked by the Succeeding of the Thatcher government, who had left our classroom in dilapidated state, and I set out to create better public services for those most in need of it – regardless of whether it's been as a councillor or minister of the government.
I've never hid my policy, even during the last election.
No one in Nottingham East could in any way have been confused about my political positions and disagreements with the current leadership.
To those of those who tabled the motion for a resolution on Friday, I would say that as the country pours towards Brexit, which will damage households, businesses and our public services, I don't understand the need to waste time and energy on my loyalty to Labour's leader.
But the one message I've got isn't for Nottingham Momentum but to the local electorate, whether they're members of the Labour Party or not: I'm proud to serve you and I promise that no threats of opt-out or political aptitude will deter me from acting based on what I think is your interest.
Chris Leslie is an Opportunity Member for Nottingham East
