"The Sea Is Ours": Inland state Bolivia hopes court will reopen route to the Pacific
Sailors are patrolling around the naval headquarters that shrouded in cravings, in La Paz.
A sea-swrewed flag has hoisted on public buildings.
Naval bases from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon River are overpainted with the motto: "The sea is ours, it's a right.
"It's a duty to take it back.'
In the entire inland state of Bolivia, the memory of a stretch of coastline lost to Chile in a bloody Conflict of Resources in the 19th-century is still strong, and so is the longing to sail on the Pacific again.
The aspirations are perhaps at their peak for several decades, while Bolivia is awaiting a ruling from the International Court of Justice on October 1, after five years of deliberation.
"Bolivia has the momentum, a sense of community and clarity, and, of course, waiting with a positive look at the outcompaniment," said Roberto Calzadilla, a bolivian diplomat.
Many bolivians will see the decision of the International Judiciary on big screens across the country and hope that the Judgment in The The Hague will uphold Bolivia that, after decades of deliberations, Chile is obliged to negotiate granting Bolivia sovereignty over an area with access to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic indigenous president, who is faced with a controversial election campaign next year, also has a lot at stake at the judiciary on Monday.
"We're close to returning to the Pacific," he pledged at the end of August.
But the analysts believe it is unlikely that the court will take Bolivia's party - and that there wouldn't be any major changes if it did.
The United Nations Assembly, which has its seat in the Netherlands, does not have the power to overheat Chilean territory and has stipulated that it cannot determine the outcome of possible deliberations.
That the International Court of First Instance ruled just six months after the conclusive anguations were heard indicates that the case "was not complicated," said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert in international law.
And instead of advancing Bolivia's cause, over the past four years may have relapsed.
"The cause of access to the sea has been hijacked by the current Bolivian leadership," Zárate said.
Morales' belligerent rhetoric has rid of the remaining Chilean benevolence, she said.
Bolivia and Chile will at some point continue the discussions, but it will become extremely difficult to discuss after this.
The two countries haven't exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court's decision-making was abnormally quick.
On Monday, Bolivia will get "an extraordinary opportunity to open up a new era of relations with Chile" and a chance to "compezel an era of 139-year-old erupt with disagreements and achieve benefits for both parties," he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, who is still one of Latin America's most popular presidents, used the maritime problem as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never give up the right of access to the Pacific," he added.
Judgment is an opportunity to see that we've overcoming the past."
