John Edward: Language Skills Are Essential for Global Citizens
Scotland's independent schools have a lot of great academic results behind them, and it has continued in 2018 with yet another series of outstanding exam results, which are even strengthened by individual and collective success in sports, arts, music and other endeavors in the local community.
With up to 30,000 puppies around Scotland, these schools represented by The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) are striving to deliver the best level of service to their pupils and parents.
The independent schools are attempting to prepare their pupils for further and higher-education, their chosen career and their place as global citizens.
As an education sector that can put together and decompatriate a bespoke curriculum, we find that modern languages remain a popular topic in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you're talking to a man in a language he understands, it hits the head.
"If you talk to him in his own language, it hits the heart."
It's an important reminder that we can't just stick to English when we want to build relationships and trust among people from other countries.
We can look at the latest exam results this year that languages are at the top in the statistics with the most passed exams in the independent schools.
A total of 68 per cent of pupils who studied foreign languages got the high grade A.
The data, collected by SCIS's 74 member schools, showed that 72 per cent of pupils gained the grade A in mandarin, while 72 per cent of those who studied German, 69 per cent of those who studied French, and 63 per cent of those who studied Spanish also received an A.
It shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as important competences, which children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Linguistics are now perceived as subject selection at the same level as STEM (Security, Technique, Engineering and Physics) in independent schools’ pensa and elsewhere.
A survey conducted by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills carried out in 2014 found that among the reasons why employers failed to fill vacancies had 17% with a lack of language proficiency.
That's why language proficiencies are becoming more and more important in preparing young people for their future careers.
More job opportunities in the future will require language proficiency, and these skills are essential in a globalised world.
No matter what career the young people choose, if they have learned a different language, they have a great advantage in the future with a lifelong proficiency like this.
Opportunity to communicate directly with people from other countries automatically gives a multilingual person a head start to competitors.
According to one YouGov poll with over 4,000 respondents in the UK in 2013, 75 per cent couldn't speak a foreign language well enough to hold a conversation, and French is the only language spoken by a multi-digit percentage, 15 per cent.
That's why it's important to invest in language teaching for those who are children today.
When one can speak multiple languages, especially languages from developing countries, give children a better chance of finding meaningful work.
In Scotland, there are schools between differences in which languages are taught.
A number of schools are focusing on the more classic modern languages, while others teach languages that are expected to be most important to the UK when looking ahead to 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
No matter what your child is interested in, there will always be a number of languages to choose from in independent schools with educators who are specialists in the field.
Scottish independent schools are dedicated to delivering a learning environment that prepares children and provides them with the skills required to be successful no matter what the future holds.
At this stage it cannot deny that languages continue to be really important for the country's future in a global business environment, and this must be reflected in the degree programmes.
Modern languages should in fact be regarded as "international communication proficiencies".
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, this diversity and this accolade to the youth of Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is the leader of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
