It's only happened twice in Ryder Cup-history that a team has struggled back after being behind by four points before the singles matches. Since the Furyks currently have the Cup, however, they only have to obtain a draw to keep it.
After being second-best for two-floors, however, it seems they can't master a counter-at-at-attack on Sunday.
North Korea says they won't regreassed unilaterally if there's no confidence
North Korea's secretary of state on Saturday told the United Nations that continuing sanctions amplify their misconfidence in the United States, and that it will not abandon their nuclear weapons unilaterally in such conditions.
Ri Yong Ho recounted the General Assethrenus General Assembly that North Korea had exhibited "significant benevolence" within the past year, for example by stopping nuclear and missile tests, dismantling an area for nuclear tests and laws not developing nuclear weapons and atomic technology.
"However, we haven't seen a similar response from the United States," he said.
"Without reassurance to the United States, there is no confidence in our national security and in such circumstances there is no chance that we ourselves will take first steps and disrench unilaterally."
While Ri echoed well-familiar North Korean grievance about Washington's resistance to a "phase-divided" approach to nuclear disarmament, in which North Korea would be rewarded if they took gradual steps. But his speech seemed important, as it has not unequivocally refuted unilateral disarmament, as has otherwise previously been the case from Pyongyang.
Ri referred to a joint announcement from Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first-time summit between a seated U.S. president and a North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, in which Kim pledged to work "nuclear disarmament on the Korean peninsula," while Trump vowed to guarantee North Korea's security.
North Korea has demanded a formal end to the Korean War, which played out in 1950-53, but the United States has said Pyongyang must first abandon their nuclear weapons.
Washington has also shown reluctance to bleed tough international sanctions on North Korea.
"The United States insists on 'nuclear disarmament first' and deepens the pressure of sanctions in order to achieve their purpose by coercion and even opposes 'declaration of the end of the war'," Ri said.
"The perception that the sanctions can bring us to our knees is a wishdream among people who don't know who we are.
But the problem is that the continuing sanctions amplify our distrust.”
Ri didn't mention plans for a summit any more between Kim and Trump, as the U.S. leader highlighted at the U.N. earlier this week.
The minister instead highlighted three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in within the past five months, adding: "If the nuclear disarmament were to be negotiated with South Korea and not the U.S., negotiations wouldn't be so deadlocked."
Nevertheless, the tone of Rice speech was very different from last year, in which he recounted U.N. General Assembly that firing of North Korea's rockets against US mainland was inevitable, after "Mr Evil President" Trump called Kim a "rocket man" on a suicidal mission.
Last year, Trump at the United Nations wreamed "total annihilation" of North Korea, but this year he commended Kim for extinguishing in relation to disarmament but said there's still a lot that needs to be done and that the sanctions have to remain until North Korea crashes.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn't have a timetable for these moves, but said "It doesn't matter if it takes two years, three years or five months."
China and Russia believe U.N. security council should reward Pyongyang for the steps taken.
U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo, however, told UN Security Council on Thursday that: "The Security Council must uphold the sanctions with conviction and without fail until we achieve the total, final, confirmed nuclear disarmament."
Since 2006, the Security Security Council has unanimously heightened economic sanctions against North Korea in an effort to halt Pyongyang's missile and nuclear weapons program.
Pompeo met with Ri on the sidelines during the United Nations General Assembly and subsequently said he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for another meeting.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times throughout the year, but his last trip did not go well.
He left Pyongyang in July and said there had been stride, while North Korea just hours later accused him of making "gangster-like demands."
At a meeting with the Moon this month, North Korea pledged to shut down a missile plant as well as a nuclear companiment if the United States took "similar steps."
He said Kim had told him that the "similar steps he demanded were the security vows that Trump gave in Singapore, as well as movements towards a normalization of the relationship with Washington.
Students at Harvard take course about getting enough sleep
A new course at the Harvard University has given all undergraduate students more sleep. The aim of the course is to oppose the growing macho-culture, where you study throughout the night held up by large amounts of caffeine.
A researcher has shown that the students at the world's best university often don't know the first thing about taking care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at the Harvard Medical School and specialist at the Brigham and Women's Hospital, has made the course, which he thinks is the first of its kind in the United States.
He got the idea for the course after giving a presentation on what effects sleep deprivation has on learning.
"When the presentation was finished, a girl came up to me and said: "Why don't I only get to know this now when I'm on the nominee?"
"She said that no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep, which surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
The course, offered for the first time this year, the students explains how good sleeping habits can improve one's academic and sporting achievement as well as one's overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard Medical School and managing director of the university's health care area, says the university has decided to offer the course after discovering that the students were in severe sleep deprivation over the course of the week.
The hour-long course consists, among other things, of various interactive exercises.
In one of the exercises there is a picture of a dorm room in which the students have to click on coffee cups, curtains, running shoes and books to gain knowledge about the effects of caffeine and light, and how one's sporting performance is affected by sleep deprivation, and how important good sleeping habits are.
In another part of the course, participants hear about how sleep deprivation over a long period of time increases the risk of heart attacks, blood clots, depression and cancer.
Participants are urged to think about their day-to-day habits by clicking on different icons on an interactive map of the campus.
"We know it won't change student behavior right away.
"But we think they have the right to know - just as one has the right to know how cigarettes affect health," adds Professor Czeisler.
"The pride of taking an "allnighter" still exists, he says, adding that modern technology and increased pressure on the pupils have caused sleep deprivation is a growing problem.
"Secret weapon" against stress, exhaustion and anxiety is good and adequate sleep, he said - it even counteracts weight gain because sleep deprivation causes the brain to think that one is starving, which is why they are constantly starvous.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old californier who studies chemical and physical biology, last-year helped Professor Czeisler organize the course as he took one of his courses on his first-greeting year at Harvard.
He said the course had reopened his eyes and led him to work on turning it into a general course throughout the university.
He hopes that the next will be for all graduates to be offered a similar course before continuing their education.
Professor Czeisler advises that students set an alarm so that they know when to go to bed, and when to get up. This can create awareness of the harmful effects of the light from electronic screens, which can jerk at the circadian rhythm and make it harder to fall asleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Goals from Menga lower Gerrard and co.
