Yearbook: The Invent Inventor of the Geiger Counter
And now a page from yearbook "Sunday Morning": September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today – if we ARE... – was the day when the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger invented a method of detecting and measuring radioactivity, an inauguration that should eventually lead to the apparatus known as the geiger counter.
The Geiger counter has become a staple of both the science and popular culture, as it could be seen in "Bells of Coronado" from 1950, in which Roy Rogers and Dale Evans had the mainstays as peasant scientists:
Man: "What on earth is it?"
Rogers: "It's a geiger numerator, which is used to find radioactive minerals such as uranium.
When you put on these headphones, you can actually hear the effects of the atoms that the radioactivity in the minerals is doing."
Evans: "Hold up, now it's going on!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, a few days before he would have turned 63.
But the invention, which bears his name, lives on.
New Cancer Vaccine May Teach Immune System to "See" Cancer Cells
New Cancer Vaccine May Teach the Immune System to "See" Cancer Cells and Kill Them
The vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize cancer cells as part of treatment
The method consists of extracting immune cells from a patient and changing them in the laboratory
They can then "see" the protein that's common to many cancers and where they're injected again
A test vaccine performs promisingly in patients with a number of cancers.
When a woman with ovarian cancer was treated with the vaccine, which teaches the immune system to recognize cancer cells, the cancer disappeared for more than 18 months.
The method consists of extracting immune cells from patients and altering them in the lab, so they can "see" the protein named HER2, as many cancers have, and then inject the cells again.
Professor Jay Berzofsky from the US National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, said: "Our findings suggest we have a very promising vaccine."
HER2 "makes several cancers to develop", including breast, ovarian, lung and bowel cancer, Professor Berzofsky.
A similar approach, taking immune cells out of patients and "teaching" them from attacking cancer cells has acted in the treatment of a particular type of leukemia.
