What's the Global Citizens Festival, and has it done something to reduce poverty?
On Saturday, the New York is the host city for the Global Citizens Festival – an annual music festival with a very impressive array of artists and an equally impressive goal; to put an end to the poverty of the world.
The Global Citizens Festival will for the seventh-family receive tens of thousands of people at Central Park's big lawn, where they can look forward to concerts with Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes. At the same time, the festival will raise awareness of the actual goal of ending extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival kicks off in 2012 and is an expansion of the Global Poverty Project, an international interest organization that struggles to put an end to Poverty by increasing the number of active adversaries.
In order to receive a free ticket for the event (if you don't want to pay for a VIP ticket), concert-goers have to carry out a series of assignments or "actions".
But how Successful has Global Citizen been, now that they have 12 years left to achieve the goal?
Does the idea of rewarding people with a free greet create real change, or is it just yet another case of so-called "clicktivism" - i.e. that people feel that they are making a difference by signing a retrieval on the web or making a Twitter post?
Global Citizen states that since 2011, they have recorded more than 19 million "actions" from their members targeting various targets.
They believe these actions have sprongled world leaders to make binding targets and policy actions equivalent to more than $37 billion, which by 2030 are expected to have affected more than 2.25 billion people.
At the beginning of 2018, the group listed 390 goals and measures that had come about because of their actions, which amounted to over $10-billion that had already been disbursed or collected.
The group estimates that so far these funds have had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
One of the main achievements is The Power of Nutrition, which has started in the UK and collaborating between investors and strong-occupied individuals. They striving toward "helping children live out their full potential" and promises $35 million to address nutrition issues in Rwanda after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
"With the help of the English government, donors, the governments of other countries and world-citizens like you, the social injustice with malnutrition can become a footnote in the history books," the ambassador of The Power of Nutrition Tracey Ullman told the audience at a concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that England's government has now pledged funds to improve the nutrition for mothers and children after receiving more than 5,000 incitements online. This project, the Power of Nutrition, will help five million mothers and children advising on nutrition.
In response to one of the website's frequently veiled questions that read: "What leads you to believe that we can put an end to extreme deprivation?"
write Global Citizen: "It's going to be a long and hard fight, and sometimes we'll stumble and make a mistake.
But just as with the civil rights movement and the movements against apartheid it will succeed, because we are stronger together."
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B and Janelle Monáe are among the performers at this year's festival in New York. Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman must host.
