Reher said he's doubting that Republican senator Jeff Flakes (Arizona) request for an FBI investigation in the 11th hour will soften angry electorate.
“Women won’t forget what happened yesterday – they won’t forget it tomorrow or in November,” said Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior advisor and national spokeswoman for the Progressive group MoveOn.
On Friday morning, the protesters shuddered during a demonstration in the Senate: "November is Coming!" when the Republicans, who control the judicial committee, chose to proceed with Kavanaugh's nomination notwithstanding the testimony of Professor Christine Blasey Ford," Mic said.
"Democratic enthusiasm and motivation is going to reach new altitude," independent political commentator Stu Rothenberg released.
"People say it's already reached a high level; that's right.
But it can reach even louder, especially among female swing selectors in the suburbs and younger electorate – 18-29-year-olds – who are seldom-spoken, despite not liking the president.”
Even before Ford's public allegations of the Supreme Court-nomined's sexual assault, analysts had pointed out that it could backfire if the Republicans proceeded with the nomination.
"It's got a worse mess for the Republicans," said Michael Steele, former chairman of the Republican National Committee, at the start of last week according to NBC News.
"It's not just about the committee's vote or the final outcome, or whether Kavanaugh is held accountable; it's also about how the Republicans have handled it here and how they've dealt with her," points out Guy Cecil, chairman of Priorities USA - a group that supports Democrats from being elected - to the news channel.
But the Americans seem to have shared attitudes about who to believe in after the testimonies from Ford and Kavanaugh, in which the latter have a bit more on his side.
A new poll from YouGov shows that 41% of those surveyed certainly or presumably relied on Ford's testimony, while 35% said they certainly or presumably reserving Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38% said they thought Kavanaugh either presumably or certainly lied during his testimony, while only 30% said the same thing about Ford.
At the request of Flake, the FBI is now investigating the allegations presented by the Ford and at least another, Deborah Ramirez wrote of The Guardian.
Ford recounted last-week during oath to Senate Judiciary that Kavanaugh fully assaulted her when she was 17.
Ramirez alleges that the Supreme Court nomineer showed her genitals to her at a party when they both studied at Yale in the 80's.
The Inventor of the Internet Plans to Create a New Internet and Compete with Google and Facebook
The inventor of the internet, Tim Berners-Lee, is launching a new company to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The technology legend's newest project is called Inrupt and is a company that builds on Berners-Lee's open-source platform Solid.
Solid gives users the opportunity to choose where their data is stored and who has access to what information.
In an ex-exclusive interview with the Fast Company, Berner's Lee joked that the plan with Inrupt is "world domination."
"We're going to have to do it by now," he said about the start-up business.
"It's a historic moment."
The app uses Solid's technology so people can create their own "personal online data collection" or a POD.
It can contain contact lists, to-do lists, calendars, music libraries, and other personal and professional tools.
That's, as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify were available in a single browser at the same time at the same time.
What's unique about the personal online data collection is that it's entirely up to the user to decide who can access that specific information.
The company calls it "personal empowerment through data."
The idea of Inrupt, according to the company's chief executive, John Bruce is to gather the resources, processes and abilities to make Solid available to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform bought by IBM, some software developers on the project contract and a community of volunteer programmers.
As of this week, technology developers around the world can make their own decentralized apps by using the tools available on Inrupt's website.
Berners-Lee says he and his team don't talk to "Facebook or Google about whether to change it all completely so that all their business models suddenly become redundant."
"We don't ask for their permission."
Briner-Speech at Medium last Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt's "mission is to offer commercial energy and an ecosystem, which can ensure the integrity and quality of the new internet being built on Solid."
Berners-Lee changed the internet when he started the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an important womb in the so-called net-neutrality debate.
While Inrupt is launched, Berners-Lee will still be an inventor and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic about the internet's next term," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann
The English church's only pastor, who won the Victorian choir by fighting during the First World War, is celebrated in her hometown 100 years later.
Lieutenant Colonel and pastor Bernard Wann won the order on September 29, 1918, under the attack at Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
But he was killed four days later by a sniper and was never told that he had won the highest English military order.
On Saturday a memorial stone was unveiled by his two grandchildren at a parade in Rushden, Northhamptonshire.
Michael Vann, one of his grandchildren, said it was "incredibly symbolic" that the stone was unveiled exactly 100 years after his grandfather's order-deserving accomplishment.
According to the London Gazette, Lt Lender Vann on September 29, 1918 led his battalion over Canal de Saint-Quentin "through a very thick fog and during hefty shelling from machine gun and artillery."
He later raced forward to the front line and led with the "great-squeror" of his people forward, before solely attacking an artillery and decapitating three cannons.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918 – just a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann at 72 said that his grandfather's actions were "something I knew I could never live up to, but which makes me humbling."
He and his brother James Vann also put a wreath after the parade, which was led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann stated that he "feeled very honored to take part in the parade", adding that "the support of all these people underscores the true heroism."
