We settle for five games instead of six as we usually do. We were trying to give our fans something extra and then we went over time.
"It's an unlucky situation."
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley wrecked "necessity" over sexuality
Tom Daley says he felt worth less than everyone in his upbringing because of his sexuality, but it motivated him to be successful.
The 24-year-old said it didn't dawn on him until he got in the larger classes that "everyone's not like me."
When he's on Radio 4's first Desert Island Discs with Lauren Laverne, he said he was talking about the rights of homosexuals to give others "hope."
He also said that he went less into winning the Olympics after becoming a parent.
Unusual host on the radio program who has been running for a long time, Kirsty Young, has taken time off a few months due to illness.
Daley was a guest in Laverne's first program and said he felt "smaller" than everyone else in his upbringing because "it wasn't socially accepted to like boys and girls."
He said: "Until today, the feelings of feeling worthy of memory and feeling different have been the things that have given me the strength to succeed."
"He wanted to prove he was "something," he said so he didn't disappoint everyone when they got to know his sexuality.
The dual Olympal bronze winner has become a high-profile LGBT campaigner and used his attendance at this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to appeal for a decriminalisation of homosexuality in several countries.
"He said he spoke out because he felt lucky to be able to live openly with no negative consequences, and he would give others "hope."
The three-time world champion said he had been "surprised" when he fell in love with a man - American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, which he met in 2013.
Daley remarried last-year-wrenching the Oscar-winner, who is 20 years older than him, but recounted that the age difference had never been an issue.
He said it is difficult to find someone of the same age who has experienced similar ups and downs when you go through so much at such a young age – he went to his first OLYMPs as a 14-year-old, and his father died of cancer three years later.
The couple joined a son named Robert Ray Black-Daley in June and Daley said "whole his perspective" had changed.
"If you'd asked me last year, it'd all be whether I should win a gold medal," he said.
"But there are more important things than Olympic gold medals.
"My Olympic gold medal is Robbie."
His son has the same name as his father, Robert, who died in 2011 as a 40-year-old after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley recounted that his father didn't accept him going to die, and one of the last things he asked about was whether they'd been given tickets to London 2012 - when he wanted to sit in the front row.
'I couldn't tell him he wasn't going to be there by then.
"I held his hand when his breathing stopped and it first dawned on me that he wasn't immortal when he stopped shaking and was dead," he said.
The following year Daley took part in the 2012 Olympics and won bronze.
"I just knew that's what I'd been dreaming about all my life - to dive in front of a home crowd at the Olympics. There wasn't anything that could beat that feeling," he said.
It was also the inspiration for his first song selection – Proud by Heather Small – who had meant a lot during the run-up to the Olympics and still gave him goosebumps.
The Desert Island Discs run on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11.15 a.m. BST.
