Germany's new warship relaspised
German navy's newest frigate should have stood ready in 2014 to replace warships from the Cold War. But the Germans have to wait at least one more year due to technical problems and an e-scaling price.
The inauguration of the largest ship in the all-new Baden-Wuerttemberg class of fregat, "Rheinland-Pfalz", has been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to Die Zeit, circulating a military spokesperson.
The ship should have become part of the fleet in 2014, but post-delivery problems have tormented the ambitious project.
The four ships of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class, which the fleet commissioned back in 2007, is to replace the aging frigates from the Bremen-class.
About them you know they want a powerful cannon, a huge range of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles as well as stealth technologies such as reduced radar, in-frared and acoustic signatures.
Another important feature for the newest frigates is a lesser need for maintenance: It should be possible to have frigates away from the home ports for up to two years.
But the reprehensive deflicts mean that the advanced warships—repeatedly cause Germany to emerge as an overseas force—will already be obsessive when they are deployed.
Unsuccessful F125 freat made headlines last year when the German navy officially refused to take the ship into use and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipwreck in Hamburg.
It was the first time the fleet delivered a ship back to the shipbuilder after delivery.
We did not know much about the reasons for the repatriation, but the German media mentioned a number of crucial "software and hardware failures", which made the warship unusable in combat situations.
Software flaws were of particular importance, as ships from the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class must be controlled by a crew of 120 sailors – that’s only half the crew from the older Bremen frigates.
It also turned out that the ship was far too heavy, which reduced its performance and limited the fleet's opportunity for future updates.
The 7000-two "Rheinland-Pfalz" is said to be twice as heavy as similar ships used by the Germans during the Second World War.
In addition to hardware flaws, the cost of the project, which includes training the crew, is also becoming an issue.
It is said to have reached a stamming $3.1 billion (3.6 billion), with an initial cost of €2.2 billion.
The problems with the newest frigates become even more important in the light of recent warnings that Germany's naval power is shrugging.
Hans-Peter Bartels, commander of the German government's defence committee, admitted earlier this year the navy "kempts out of preoccupied ships."
The official said that the problem has grown over time because the old ships were taken out of use without being replaced.
He complained that none of Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates were poked to become part of the fleet.
National Trust snig listens to the Secret Life of Bats
New research from an area in the Scottish highlands is trying to reveal how bats use the landscape when chamowing.
Recollection is that the finds will shed light on the behavior of the unique, flying mammals and will help in the work of future conservation efforts.
The study is carried out by scientists from Scotland's National Trust and follows common bats, dwarf flakes, long-eyed flaked bats and water flakes at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Particular recording equipment is placed at carefully selected locations in the area to record the activities of the bats throughout the season.
Data is also being collected from NTS and volunteers using hand-held detectors.
In-depth analyzing analyses of the audio recordings will unveil the flakes' frequencies and show which species does what.
In order to produce a detailed landscape picture of their behavior, a report and a map of the habitat are made.
Nature conservation advisor for NTS Rob Dewar hopes that the results reveal which areas of the habitat are most important and how they are used by the different species.
This knowledge can help identify the benefits of habitat conservation interventions such as the establishment of meadows, and how best to conserve woodlands for bats and similar species.
The number of bats in Scotland and Britain has fallen drastically over the past 100 years.
They are threatened by buildings and urban expansion projects, which affect shipowners and curtail their habitat.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a danger. So can fly traps, chemical treatment of building materials and attacks from pets.
Flatter mice aren't actually blind.
But because they mainly hunt at night, their ears are more workable than their eyes.
They use an advanced eccholalization technique to find insects and fly bypassing hurdles.
NTS, which is responsible for the preservation of more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 acres of land around the country, takes bats very seriously.
They have ten-trained experts, who often collect data, inspect nests and sometimes make rescues.
The organization has even created Scotland's first and only dedicated bat sanctuary at the area around Threave in Dumfries and Galloway, home to eight out of ten of Scotland's bat species.
The area takes care, David Thompson, says it's an ideal territory for them.
"Here at Threave we have a really good area for bat," he said.
"We've got old buildings, a lot of old trees and the whole good habitat.
“There’s a lot you don’t know about flakes, so the work here and elsewhere can help to understand what they need in order to thrive.”
He underscores the importance of looking for flakes before doing maintenance tasks on real estate, as the destruction of a single nest can kill up to 400 females and juveniles, thereby risk extinguishing an entire local population.
Bats are protected and it is illegal to kill, inconvenience or disturb them or their nests.
Elisabeth Ferrell, the Scottish project manager for the Bat Conservation Trust, has urged the public to lend a hand.
She said: "We still have a lot, we need to learn about our bats, and for many of the species we have, we don't know how the populations develop."
