"We cry when we get angry," Steinem told me 45 years later.
"I don't think that's abnormal. Do you think?"
She continued: "I got great backing from a woman who was the boss, who said that she also cried when she got angry, but that she developed a technique, which meant that when she got angry and started crying, she'd say to the one she was talking to: "You might think I'm upset because I'm crying.
“But I’m angry.”
And then she just kept going.
And I figured it was brilliant.”
Tears are allowed as an expression of anger, in part, because they are essentially misunderstood.
One of my clearest memories is from one of my first jobs in an octopus with a predominance of men, where I often cried with anger, where I was taken by the neck of an elderly woman – a cold boss that I had always been a little afraid of – and pulled out on the stairs.
"Never let them see that you're crying," she told me.
'They don't know you're enraged.
"They'll think you're sorry and they're going to feel good that they't got you."
Patricia Schroeder, who at that time was a member of Congress for the Democrats of Colorado, had worked with Gary Hart in his presidential election campaigns.
In 1987, when Hart was unveiled in having an affair on a boat named Monkey Business and withdrew as a candidate, Schroeder was really frustrated and came to the conclusion that she might just as well run as a presidential contender.
"It wasn't a very well-thought-out decision," she laughingly told me 30 years later.
"There were already seven other contenders in the run-up, and they certainly didn't need yet another nominee.
Some called it 'SnowWhite and the Seven dwarves'."
Because it's late in the election campaign, she was lagging behind in fundraising, so she promised she won't participate unless she collected $2 million.
The battle was lost in advance.
"She discovered that some of her supporters, who gave $1,000 to men, only gave her $250.
"Do they think I'm going to get a discount?" she thought.
“When she gave a speech that she didn’t want to run, she was overwhelmed with emotion—grass of the people who backed her, frustration at the system that made it so hard-moving money and reaching out to voters instead of electoriences, and annoyance at sexism—that she got a lump in her throat.
"You'd think I've had a nerve concoction," Schroeder recalled how the press reacted to her.
"You'd think I was sponsored by Kleenex.
I can remember thinking, what are you going to write on my tombstone?
"She was crying?"
That's how the trade war between the U.S. and China could be an advantage for Beijing
The opening ointments in the trade war between the United States and China were loud, and even though the battle is largely over, ridiction between the countries can be an advantage for Beijing in the long run, experts say.
U.S. President Donald Trump fissed the first warning shot earlier this year by putting tariffs on key Chinese export goods such as solar panels, steel and aluminium.
Biggest escalation this week, with new customs duties affecting goods worth $200 billion, applies, in principle, to half of all goods imported into the United States from China.
Beijing has each time retaliated, most recently by putting tariffs of 5-10% on U.S. $60-billion.
China sworn to follow the United States all the way, and the world's second-largest economy probably won't give way.
If it causes Washington to retract its demands, it means that they give in, but for Chinese President Xi Jinping it will be overly embarrassing to bend to the United States.
However, experts say that if Beijing plays its cards right, the U.S. squeeze in the trade war could have a positive effect on China in the long run by making the two markets less reliant on each other.
"A quick decision in either Washington or Beijing can create a fertile ground for an economy that can get out of control in both countries, and that's actually a lot more dangerous than was thought," said Abigail Grace, a research employee focused on Asia at the think tank Center for New American Security.
Syria 'acorry' is 'premeditary' to receive refugees, says foreign minister
Syria says the country is poked for the refugees to be able to return, asking for help in building the country devastated by the more than seven-year-long war.
Speaking to the United Nations General Assembhended Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said the conditions in the country were recovering.
"Currive today the situation is more stable and safe thanks to the successful fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to have control over the areas destroyed by terrorists and is working to remake a normal state.
All conditions are now in place for refugees to be able to rejoin the country that they had to leave because of terrorists and the one-sided economic sanctions that afflicted their daily lives and livelihood.
The United Nations Estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have escaped from the country since 2011, when the war began.
Another six million people who are still in the country are in need of emergency aid.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian government gladly accepted help in rebuilding the ravaged country.
But he emphasized that the government would not accept conditional assistance or help from countries that helped support the rebellion.
Europe secures Ryder Cup victories in Paris
The European team has won Ryder Cup 2018 and beaten the US team 16.5 - 10.5 in the Le Golf National outside of Paris.
The U.S. has now lost six times in a row on European soil and hasn't won a single Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe won the trophy back when Captain Thomas Bjørn's team reached the 14.5-stroke it demanded to beat the United States.
American star Phil Mickelson, who was having a hard time in most of the tournament, shot his drive into the lake on par 3 on the 16th hole, prompting him to drop the match against Francesco Molinari.
Italian Molinari performed well in all rounds and turned 1 out of a total of 4 gamers who have got 5-0-0 since 1979, when the tournament's current format started.
American Jordan Spieth was sent out with 5&4 by the lowest-ranked European player, Danish Thorbjørn Olesen.
No. 1 in the world ranking, Dustin Johnson, fell 2 and 1 to Ian Poulter from England who may have played his last Ryder Cup.
Veteran with eight Ryder Cups in the bag, Spaniard Sergio Garcia, became the tournament's most winning European with 25.5 career points.
"I don't usually cry, but today I can't do for it.
It's been a tough year.
I'm very thankful that Thomas chose me and believed in me.
I'm really happy to get the trophy back.
"It's a team-effort, and I'm glad I could help," an emotive Garcia said after the European victory.
He passes the torch on to his compatriot John Ram, who squeezed American golf legend Tiger Woods 2&1 in a single match on Sunday.
"I am incredibly proud to have beaten Tiger Woods. I followed him as a child," said 23-year-old Rahm.
Woods lost all four matches in France and now has a Ryder Cup history of 13-21-3.
That's a quirky statistic for one of the best golfers ever to have won 14 major titles, surpassed only by Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S. team all weekend had trouble figuring the form with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who wore a solid game throughout the tournament.
US captain, Jim Furyk, said after his team's bufficient performance: "I'm proud of the guys, because they fought for the cause.
There was still time this morning when we countered Europe.
We're gonna lose.
The hat off to Thomas.
- He's an excellent captain.
All his 12 players played really well.
We want to regroup, and I'm going to collaborate with the PGA of America and our Ryder Cup committee, and then we'll move on.
I love the 12 guys and I'm proud to be their captain.
You've got to ease on the hat.
“We were defeated.”
Updating on algae blossom: Concentrating decreases in Pinella, Manatee and Sarasota
The most recent report from the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission shows that there is a general decreasing algae concentration in parts of the Tampa Bay.
According to the FWC, dissclations are reported in the area around Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier – suggesting that the concentration is declining.
The bloom of algae spreads over a coastline of 210 km from northern Pinellas County to southern Lee County.
There's less blooming about 16 km from the coast of Hillsborough County, but there are fewer than last week.
Flourises have also been observed in Pasco County.
Symbior concentrations have been reported at or at sea in Pinellas County over the last week, low to high concentrations in the sea off in Hillsborough County, background concentration to high concentrations in Manatee County, background concentration to high concentrations in or in the ocean off Sarasota County, background concentration to medium concentrations in Charlotte County, background concentration to high concentrations in or in the ocean off Lee County and low concentrations in Collier County.
Deliberation difficulties in Pinella, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier are still being reported.
No breathing difficulties have been reported in northwest Florida in the past week.
